<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-Applications-Derivatives-chapter-exercises">
  <title>3. Applications of Derivatives- Chapter Exercises</title>

<introduction>
<p>
This cumulative review module provides a comprehensive synthesis of the core applications of 
differentiation and basic antidifferentiation methods covered throughout this chapter. 
Working through these exercises will help reinforce your understanding of function optimization, 
linear curve behavior analysis, limit evaluations, and numerical root approximations.
</p>
<p>
These problems mimic standard exam configurations, prompting you to determine absolute boundaries 
on closed domains, perform complete first and second derivative graphing tests, model dynamic 
rate-of-change word problems, and seamlessly switch back and forth between derivative and integral 
operations.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercises xml:id="exercises-chapter-review">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <!-- Problem 1 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-absolute-extrema">
    <statement>
      <p> Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of each function on the given interval. </p>
    </statement>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>f(x)=x^2-1</m> on <m>[-1, 2]</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First, find the critical numbers by setting the derivative to zero:
          <me>f'(x) = 2x \implies 2x = 0 \implies x = 0</me>
          Since <m>x = 0</m> is in the interval <m>[-1, 2]</m>, evaluate <m>f(x)</m> at the critical number and the endpoints:
          <md>
            <mrow>f(0) \amp = (0)^2 - 1 = -1</mrow>
            <mrow>f(-1) \amp = (-1)^2 - 1 = 0</mrow>
            <mrow>f(2) \amp = (2)^2 - 1 = 3</mrow>
          </md>
          Comparing these values, the absolute minimum is <m>-1</m> at <m>x = 0</m>, 
          and the absolute maximum is <m>3</m> at <m>x = 2</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>g(x)=xe^{-x}</m> on <m>[-1, 1]</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Find the critical numbers using the product rule:
          <me>g'(x) = 1 \cdot e^{-x} + x \cdot (-e^{-x}) = e^{-x}(1 - x)</me>
          Setting <m>g'(x) = 0</m> gives <m>1 - x = 0 \implies x = 1</m>(since <m>e^{-x} \neq 0</m>). Evaluate <m>g(x)</m> at the boundary endpoints <m>x = -1</m> and <m>x = 1</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>g(-1) \amp = -1 \cdot e^{-(-1)} = -e \approx -2.718</mrow>
            <mrow>g(1) \amp = 1 \cdot e^{-1} = \frac{1}{e} \approx 0.368</mrow>
          </md>
          Therefore, the absolute minimum is <m>-e</m> at <m>x = -1</m>, 
          and the absolute maximum is <m>\frac{1}{e}</m> at <m>x = 1</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 2 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-find-antiderivatives">
    <statement>
      <p> Find all possible functions with the given derivative (find the general antiderivative): </p>
    </statement>
    
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>y'=2x</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Integrate using the power rule: <m>y = \int 2x \, dx = x^2 + C</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>y'=\dfrac{1}{x}</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The basic antiderivative of <m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m> is the natural logarithm function: 
          <m>y = \int \dfrac{1}{x} \, dx = \ln|x| + C</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>y'=3x^2+2x-1</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Integrate term by term:
          <me>y = \int (3x^2+2x-1) \, dx = 3\left(\frac{x^3}{3}\right) + 2\left(\frac{x^2}{2}\right) - x + C = x^3 + x^2 - x + C</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 3 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-critical-numbers">
    <introduction>
      <p> Find the critical numbers of the functions. </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>\displaystyle f(x)=\frac{1+\sin x}{1-\sin x}</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Apply the quotient rule to find <m>f'(x)</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>f'(x) \amp = \frac{(\cos x)(1 - \sin x) - (1 + \sin x)(-\cos x)}{(1 - \sin x)^2}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \frac{\cos x - \sin x \cos x + \cos x + \sin x \cos x}{(1 - \sin x)^2} = \frac{2\cos x}{(1 - \sin x)^2}</mrow>
          </md>
          Set the numerator to zero: <m>2\cos x = 0 \implies x = \dfrac{\pi}{2} + k\pi</m> for any integer <m>k</m>. 
          However, notice that when <m>x = \frac{\pi}{2} + 2k\pi</m>, <m>\sin x = 1</m>, 
          causing the original function denominator to be zero (undefined). </p>
        <p> Thus, the critical numbers are only where the derivative equals zero and the function is 
            defined: <m>x = \dfrac{3\pi}{2} + 2k\pi</m> for <m>k \in \mathbb{Z}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>K(z)=4z^3+5z^2-42z+7</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Differentiate and set equal to zero:
          <me>K'(z) = 12z^2 + 10z - 42 = 2(6z^2 + 5z - 21) = 0</me>
          Factor the quadratic binomial: <m>2(2z - 3)(3z + 7) = 0</m>. 
          The critical numbers are <m>z = \dfrac{3}{2}</m> and <m>z = -\dfrac{7}{3}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>f(x)=\sin(x)</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Differentiate and set to zero: <m>f'(x) = \cos x = 0</m>.
          The critical numbers occur at <m>x = \dfrac{\pi}{2} + k\pi</m> for all integers <m>k</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 4 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-concavity-intervals">
    <statement>
      <p> If <m>f(x) = x^3+x^2-x-1</m>, determine intervals on which the graph of <m>f</m> is concave up or down. </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
    <p> Find the first and second derivatives:
        <me>f'(x) = 3x^2 + 2x - 1 \implies f''(x) = 6x + 2</me>
        Set <m>f''(x) = 0 \implies 6x + 2 = 0 \implies x = -\frac{1}{3}</m>. 
    </p>
            <p>
          We test the intervals around the inflection point candidate <m>x = -\frac{1}{3}</m> using the sign of the second derivative:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Interval</cell>
            <cell><m>\left(-\infty, -\frac{1}{3}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(-\frac{1}{3}, \infty\right)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Test Value</cell>
            <cell><m>x = -1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 0</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Sign of <m>f''(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f''(-1) = -4 \lt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f''(0) = 2 \gt 0</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Behavior / Concavity</cell>
            <cell>Concave Down</cell>
            <cell>Concave Up</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 5 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-local-extrema-inflection">
    <statement>
      <p> If <m>f'(x) = x^4-4x^3</m>, find the local extrema of <m>f</m>. Determine the concavity and find the point of inflection. </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
  <p>
    Find critical numbers by setting the first derivative to zero: 
    <me>f'(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 = x^3(x - 4) = 0 \implies x=0, \text{ or } x=4</me>
  </p>
  <p>
    We test the intervals around our critical numbers using the first derivative sign test:
  </p>
  <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
    <row>
      <cell header="yes">Interval</cell>
      <cell><m>(-\infty, 0)</m></cell>
      <cell><m>(0, 4)</m></cell>
      <cell><m>(4, \infty)</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell header="yes">Test Value</cell>
      <cell><m>x = -1</m></cell>
      <cell><m>x = 1</m></cell>
      <cell><m>x = 5</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell header="yes">Sign of <m>f'(x)</m></cell>
      <cell><m>f'(-1) = 5 \gt 0</m></cell>
      <cell><m>f'(1) = -3 \lt 0</m></cell>
      <cell><m>f'(5) = 125 \gt 0</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell header="yes">Behavior of <m>f(x)</m></cell>
      <cell>Increasing <m>\nearrow</m></cell>
      <cell>Decreasing <m>\searrow</m></cell>
      <cell>Increasing <m>\nearrow</m></cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
  <p>
    Because the derivative changes from positive to negative at <m>x = 0</m>, 
    there is a local maximum at <m>x = 0</m>. 
    Because it changes from negative to positive at <m>x = 4</m>, there is a 
    local minimum at <m>x = 4</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now compute the second derivative for concavity: 
    <me>f''(x) = 4x^3 - 12x^2 = 4x^2(x - 3)</me>
    Setting <m>f''(x) = 0 \implies x = 0</m> or <m>x = 3</m>. We analyze the intervals across these boundary targets:
  </p>
  <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
    <row>
      <cell header="yes">Interval</cell>
      <cell><m>(-\infty, 0)</m></cell>
      <cell><m>(0, 3)</m></cell>
      <cell><m>(3, \infty)</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell header="yes">Test Value</cell>
      <cell><m>x = -1</m></cell>
      <cell><m>x = 1</m></cell>
      <cell><m>x = 4</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell header="yes">Sign of <m>f''(x)</m></cell>
      <cell><m>f'(-1) = -16 \lt 0</m></cell>
      <cell><m>f''(1) = -8 \lt 0</m></cell>
      <cell><m>f''(4) = 64 \gt 0</m></cell>
    </row>
    <row>
      <cell header="yes">Concavity</cell>
      <cell>Concave Down</cell>
      <cell>Concave Down</cell>
      <cell>Concave Up</cell>
    </row>
  </tabular>
  <p>
    Because the concavity changes signs strictly at <m>x = 3</m>, 
    the inflection point occurs at <m>x = 3</m>.
  </p>
</solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 6 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-curve-sketching">
    <introduction>
    <p> For each function below, perform a full derivative analysis to analyze its graph components:
    </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>f(x)=x^4-8x^2+1</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First, find the critical numbers by evaluating where <m>f'(x) = 0</m>:
          <me>f'(x) = 4x^3 - 16x = 4x(x^2 - 4) = 0 \implies x = 0, \, x = -2, \, \text{or } x = 2</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We test the intervals around our critical numbers using the first derivative sign test:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Interval</cell>
            <cell><m>(-\infty, -2)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-2, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 2)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(2, \infty)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Test Value</cell>
            <cell><m>x = -3</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = -1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 3</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Sign of <m>f'(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(-3) = -60 \lt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(-1) = 12 \gt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(1) = -12 \lt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(3) = 60 \gt 0</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Behavior of <m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell>Decreasing <m>\searrow</m></cell>
            <cell>Increasing <m>\nearrow</m></cell>
            <cell>Decreasing <m>\searrow</m></cell>
            <cell>Increasing <m>\nearrow</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Local minima occur at <m>x = -2</m> and <m>x = 2</m> with a value of <m>-15</m>. A local maximum occurs at <m>x = 0</m> with a value of <m>1</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Next, compute the second derivative to evaluate concavity and identify inflection points:
          <me>f''(x) = 12x^2 - 16 = 4(3x^2 - 4) = 0 \implies x = \pm \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}} \approx \pm 1.155</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We test the intervals across these structural boundary conditions:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Interval</cell>
            <cell><m>\left(-\infty, -\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(-\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}, \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}, \infty\right)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Test Value</cell>
            <cell><m>x = -2</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 2</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Sign of <m>f''(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f''(-2) = 32 \gt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f''(0) = -16 \lt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f''(2) = 32 \gt 0</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Concavity</cell>
            <cell>Concave Up</cell>
            <cell>Concave Down</cell>
            <cell>Concave Up</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Inflection points occur at <m>x = \pm \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}</m> where the coordinates are approximately <m>(\pm 1.16, -7.89)</m>.
        </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-curve-sketch-1">
          <caption>Graph of <m>f(x) = x^4 - 8x^2 + 1</m></caption>
          <image xml:id="img-curve-sketch-1" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>W-shaped polynomial curve extending through the x-axis to show real roots on both sides, with local minima at x equals plus or minus 2 and a local maximum at x equals 0.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                \begin{axis}[
                  axis lines = center,
                  xlabel = {\(x\)},
                  ylabel = {\(y\)},
                  xmin = -3.5, xmax = 3.5,
                  ymin = -18, ymax = 6,
                  xtick = {-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3},
                  ytick = {-15, -10, -5, 0, 5},
                  grid = major,
                  width = 10cm,
                  height = 8cm
                ]
                  % Plot the function extended to 2.9 to pass the x-axis (y=0) boundary smoothly
                  \addplot [
                    domain=-2.9:2.9, 
                    samples=100, 
                    color=blue, 
                    very thick
                  ] {x^4 - 8*x^2 + 1};
                  
                  % Add points
                  \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(-2,-15)} node[below] {\small \((-2, -15)\)};
                  \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(2,-15)} node[below] {\small \((2, -15)\)};
                  \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(0,1)} node[above right] {\small \((0, 1)\)};
                  \addplot[red, fill=red, mark=*] coordinates {(-1.155,-7.89)} node[left] {\small IP};
                  \addplot[red, fill=red, mark=*] coordinates {(1.155,-7.89)} node[right] {\small IP};
                \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>

      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>f(x)=x^4-x^2</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          First, find the critical numbers by setting the first derivative to zero:
          <me>f'(x) = 4x^3 - 2x = 2x(2x^2 - 1) = 0 \implies x = 0, \, x = -\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \approx -0.707, \, \text{or } x = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \approx 0.707</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We test the intervals around our critical numbers using the first derivative sign test:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Interval</cell>
            <cell><m>\left(-\infty, -\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}, 0\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(0, \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}, \infty\right)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Test Value</cell>
            <cell><m>x = -1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = -0.5</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 0.5</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Sign of <m>f'(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(-1) = -2 \lt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(-0.5) = 0.5 \gt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(0.5) = -0.5 \lt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(1) = 2 \gt 0</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Behavior of <m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell>Decreasing <m>\searrow</m></cell>
            <cell>Increasing <m>\nearrow</m></cell>
            <cell>Decreasing <m>\searrow</m></cell>
            <cell>Increasing <m>\nearrow</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Local minima occur at <m>x = \pm \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</m> with a value of <m>-\dfrac{1}{4} = -0.25</m>. A local maximum occurs at <m>x = 0</m> with a value of <m>0</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Next, compute the second derivative to evaluate concavity and identify inflection points:
          <me>f''(x) = 12x^2 - 2 = 2(6x^2 - 1) = 0 \implies x = \pm \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \approx \pm 0.408</me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We test the intervals across these boundary conditions:
        </p>
        <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Interval</cell>
            <cell><m>\left(-\infty, -\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}, \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}\right)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}, \infty\right)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Test Value</cell>
            <cell><m>x = -1</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>x = 1</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Sign of <m>f''(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f''(-1) = 10 \gt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f''(0) = -2 \lt 0</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f''(1) = 10 \gt 0</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell header="yes">Concavity</cell>
            <cell>Concave Up</cell>
            <cell>Concave Down</cell>
            <cell>Concave Up</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        <p>
          Inflection points occur at <m>x = \pm \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}</m> where the coordinates are exactly <m>\left(\pm \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{6}}, -\dfrac{5}{36}\right) \approx (\pm 0.41, -0.14)</m>.
        </p>
        
        <figure xml:id="fig-curve-sketch-2">
          <caption>Graph of <m>f(x) = x^4 - x^2</m></caption>
          <image xml:id="img-curve-sketch-2" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>W-shaped polynomial curve centered at the origin, showing local minima at x equals plus or minus 0.707 and a local maximum at the origin. The curve crosses the x-axis at x equals plus or minus 1.</shortdescription>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
                \begin{axis}[
                  axis lines = center,
                  xlabel = {\(x\)},
                  ylabel = {\(y\)},
                  xmin = -1.6, xmax = 1.6,
                  ymin = -0.6, ymax = 1.2,
                  xtick = {-1.5, -1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5},
                  ytick = {-0.5, 0, 0.5, 1},
                  grid = major,
                  width = 10cm,
                  height = 8cm
                ]
                  % Plot the function extended to 1.22 to pass the x-axis roots smoothly
                  \addplot [
                    domain=-1.22:1.22, 
                    samples=100, 
                    color=blue, 
                    very thick
                  ] {x^4 - x^2};
                  
                  % Add points
                  \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(-0.707,-0.25)} node[below] {\small \((-\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}, -\dfrac{1}{4})\)};
                  \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(0.707,-0.25)} node[below] {\small \((\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}, -\dfrac{1}{4})\)};
                  \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(0,0)} node[above right] {\small \((0, 0)\)};
                  \addplot[red, fill=red, mark=*] coordinates {(-0.408,-0.139)} node[left] {\small IP};
                  \addplot[red, fill=red, mark=*] coordinates {(0.408,-0.139)} node[right] {\small IP};
                \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </solution>
    </task>

    




  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 7 -->
<exercise xml:id="ex-optimization-semicircle">
    <statement>
      <p> A rectangle is to be inscribed in a semicircle of radius 2. What is the largest area the rectangle can have? </p>
      
      <figure xml:id="fig-inscribed-rectangle-semicircle">
        <caption>Rectangle inscribed inside a semicircle of radius 2.</caption>
        <image xml:id="img-inscribed-rectangle-semicircle" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>A mathematically perfect semicircle of radius 2 centered at the origin on an x-y coordinate plane, with an inscribed rectangle. The rectangle has width 2x and height y, with its top two vertices touching the curve of the semicircle.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5]
              % Draw grid for clear proportion check
              \draw[lightgray, dashed] (-2.2,-0.2) grid (2.2,2.2);

              % Draw axes
              \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-2.5, 0) -- (2.5, 0) node[right] {\(x\)};
              \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0, -0.3) -- (0, 2.5) node[above] {\(y\)};
              
              % Draw perfect Semicircle: radius 2 from 0 to 180 degrees
              \draw[very thick, blue] (2,0) arc (0:180:2);
              
              % Label radius intercepts
              \node[below left] at (-2, 0) {\small \(-2\)};
              \node[below right] at (2, 0) {\small \(2\)};
              \node[above left] at (0, 2) {\small \(2\)};
              
              % Exact optimal vertex positions on circle of radius 2 at 45 degrees 
              % x = 2*cos(45) = sqrt(2) approx 1.414
              % y = 2*sin(45) = sqrt(2) approx 1.414
              \coordinate (A) at ({-2*cos(45)}, 0);
              \coordinate (B) at ({2*cos(45)}, 0);
              \coordinate (C) at ({2*cos(45)}, {2*sin(45)});
              \coordinate (D) at ({-2*cos(45)}, {2*sin(45)});
              
              % Draw Shaded Inscribed Rectangle
              \draw[thick, red, fill=red!10] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- cycle;
              
              % Label dimensions and vertices
              \node[below] at (0.7, 0) {\small \(x\)};
              \node[below] at (-0.7, 0) {\small \(x\)};
              \draw[&lt;-&gt;, gray] ({2*cos(45)+0.2}, 0) -- ({2*cos(45)+0.2}, {2*sin(45)}) node[midway, right] {\(y\)};
              
              % Mark the vertex touching the circle
              \filldraw[black] (C) circle (1.5pt) node[above right] {\small \((x, y)\)};
              \filldraw[black] (D) circle (1.5pt);
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let the base of the rectangle lie along the <m>x</m>-axis, centered at the origin, with upper vertices touching the upper semicircle equation <m>y = \sqrt{4-x^2}</m>. The total base width is <m>2x</m> and the height is <m>y</m>.
        <me>A(x) = 2xy = 2x\sqrt{4-x^2}</me>
        Differentiate <m>A(x)</m> with respect to <m>x</m> using the product rule:
        <me>A'(x) = 2\sqrt{4-x^2} + 2x \cdot \left(\dfrac{-x}{\sqrt{4-x^2}}\right) = \dfrac{2(4-x^2) - 2x^2}{\sqrt{4-x^2}} = \dfrac{8 - 4x^2}{\sqrt{4-x^2}}</me>
        Set <m>A'(x) = 0 \implies 8 - 4x^2 = 0 \implies x^2 = 2 \implies x = \sqrt{2}</m>.
        The largest area is <m>A(\sqrt{2}) = 2(\sqrt{2})\sqrt{4 - 2} = 2\sqrt{2}\sqrt{2} = 4</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
</exercise>

  <!-- Problem 8 -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-optimization-parabola">
    <statement>
      <p> A rectangle has its base on the <m>x</m>-axis and its upper two vertices on the parabola 
        <m>y=12-x^2</m>. What is the largest area the rectangle can have, and what are its dimensions? </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-inscribed-rectangle-parabola">
        <caption>Rectangle inscribed underneath the parabola <m>y = 12 - x^2</m>.</caption>
        <image xml:id="img-inscribed-rectangle-parabola" width="60%">
          <shortdescription>A downward-opening parabola y equals 12 minus x squared centered on an axis system, with an inscribed rectangle. The rectangle has width 4 and height 8, with its top two vertices touching the curve of the parabola at x equals plus or minus 2.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5]
              \begin{axis}[
                axis lines = center,
                xlabel = {\(x\)},
                ylabel = {\(y\)},
                % Lowered the y-axis label position slightly down and to the left
                ylabel style={at={(axis description cs:0.5,0.98)}, anchor=north east},
                xmin = -4.5, xmax = 4.5,
                ymin = -2, ymax = 14,
                xtick = {-4, -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4},
                ytick = {-2, 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12},
                grid = major,
                width = 10cm,
                height = 8cm
              ]
                % Plot the Parabola y = 12 - x^2
                \addplot [
                  domain=-3.7:3.7, 
                  samples=100, 
                  color=blue, 
                  very thick
                ] {12 - x^2};
                
                % Draw the optimal inscribed rectangle (x = 2, y = 8)
                % Path: (-2,0) -> (2,0) -> (2,8) -> (-2,8) -> cycle
                \draw[thick, red, fill=red!10] (axis cs:-2,0) -- (axis cs:2,0) -- (axis cs:2,8) -- (axis cs:-2,8) -- cycle;
                
                % Highlight and label the key curve vertex points
                \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(2,8)} node[above right] {\small \((x, y)\)};
                \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(-2,8)};
                \addplot[black, fill=black, mark=*] coordinates {(0,12)} node[above right] {\small \(12\)};
                
                % Add dimension indicators for x and y
                \node[above] at (axis cs:1,0) {\small \(x\)};
                \node[above] at (axis cs:-1,0) {\small \(x\)};
                \node[right, gray] at (axis cs:2,4) {\small \(y\)};
                
              \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The base of the rectangle spans from <m>-x</m> to <m>x</m>, giving a width of <m>2x</m>. The height is given by <m>y = 12-x^2</m>.
        <me>A(x) = 2x(12 - x^2) = 24x - 2x^3</me>
        Differentiate and locate critical roots:
        <me>A'(x) = 24 - 6x^2 = 0 \implies 6x^2 = 24 \implies x^2 = 4 \implies x = 2</me>
        The dimensions are width = <m>4</m> and height = <m>12 - (2)^2 = 8</m>. 
        The maximum area is <m>A(2) = 4 \times 8 = 32</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
  
  <!-- Problem 9 -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-opt-box">
    <statement>
      <p> 
        An open-top rectangular box is constructed from a 10-in.-by-10-in. piece of cardboard by cutting squares of equal side length <m>x</m> from the corners and folding up the sides. Find analytically the dimensions of the box of largest volume and the maximum volume. 
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" valigns="bottom bottom" margins="auto">
        <image xml:id="img-box-layout">
          <shortdescription>A square sheet of cardboard with corners marked as small squares of side length x. Dashed lines indicate where the flaps fold up to form a box with total width 10 in.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.45]
              % Draw main 8x8 cardboard grid body
              \draw[thick, black] (0,0) rectangle (8,8);
              
              % Draw cutout corner squares
              \draw[thick, fill=gray!20] (0,0) rectangle (1.5,1.5);
              \draw[thick, fill=gray!20] (0,8) rectangle (1.5,6.5);
              \draw[thick, fill=gray!20] (8,0) rectangle (6.5,1.5);
              \draw[thick, fill=gray!20] (8,8) rectangle (6.5,6.5);
              
              % Draw dashed folding internal boundaries
              \draw[dashed, blue, thick] (1.5,1.5) -- (1.5,6.5);
              \draw[dashed, blue, thick] (6.5,1.5) -- (6.5,6.5);
              \draw[dashed, blue, thick] (1.5,1.5) -- (6.5,1.5);
              \draw[dashed, blue, thick] (1.5,6.5) -- (6.5,6.5);
              
              % Label the variable cutout size x
              \node at (0.75,0.75) {$x$};
              
              % FIXED: Changed raw XML entity &lt;-&gt; to standard LaTeX syntax
              \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (0, -0.4) -- (8, -0.4);
              \node[below] at (4, -0.4) {10 in.};
              
              % FIXED: Changed raw XML entity &lt;-&gt; to standard LaTeX syntax
              \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (-0.4, 0) -- (-0.4, 8);
              \node[left] at (-0.4, 4) {10 in.};
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>   
          
        <image xml:id="img-fold-box-3d">
          <shortdescription>A 3D perspective drawing of an open-top rectangular box with height x and base dimensions labeled 10 minus 2x.</shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55]
              % Coordinates for 3D perspective box projection
              % Front Face
              \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
              \coordinate (B) at (5,0);
              \coordinate (C) at (5,2);
              \coordinate (D) at (0,2);
              
              % Back Face corners
              \coordinate (E) at (2,1.5);
              \coordinate (F) at (7,1.5);
              \coordinate (G) at (7,3.5);
              \coordinate (H) at (2,3.5);
              
              % Fill/Draw panels from back to front for proper occlusion
              \fill[blue!5, draw=blue!50] (E) -- (F) -- (G) -- (H) -- cycle; % Interior Back Wall
              \fill[blue!10, draw=blue!50] (A) -- (D) -- (H) -- (E) -- cycle; % Interior Left Wall
              \fill[blue!12, draw=blue!50] (B) -- (C) -- (G) -- (F) -- cycle; % Interior Right Wall
              \fill[blue!15, draw=blue!40] (A) -- (B) -- (F) -- (E) -- cycle; % Base floor panel
              
              % Outer structural frame drawing
              \draw[thick, black] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- cycle; % Front Panel outline
              \draw[thick, black] (C) -- (G);
              \draw[thick, black] (D) -- (H);
              \draw[thick, black] (G) -- (H);
              
              % Dotted internal corner seam hidden from sight lines
              \draw[dashed, gray] (A) -- (E) -- (F);
              \draw[dashed, gray] (E) -- (H);
              
              % FIXED: Changed raw XML entity &lt;-&gt; to standard LaTeX syntax
              \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (-0.3,0) -- (-0.3,2);
              \node[left] at (-0.3,1) {$x$}; % Height
              
              % FIXED: Changed raw XML entity &lt;-&gt; to standard LaTeX syntax
              \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (0,-0.4) -- (5,-0.4);
              \node[below] at (2.5,-0.4) {\small $10-2x$}; % Length
              
              % FIXED: Changed raw XML entity &lt;-&gt; to standard LaTeX syntax
              \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (5.4,-0.2) -- (7.2,1.2);
              \node[below right] at (6.3,0.5) {\small $10-2x$}; % Width
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The box length is <m>10-2x</m>, width is <m>10-2x</m>, and height is <m>x</m>.
        <me>V(x) = x(10-2x)^2 = x(100 - 40x + 4x^2) = 4x^3 - 40x^2 + 100x</me>
        Find the derivative and set it to zero:
        <me>V'(x) = 12x^2 - 80x + 100 = 4(3x^2 - 20x + 25) = 4(3x - 5)(x - 5) = 0</me>
        This yields roots <m>x = 5</m> (which cuts away the entire piece of cardboard) or <m>x = \dfrac{5}{3}</m>. 
        The optimal corner cut length is <term><m>x = \dfrac{5}{3}</m> in.</term> The base dimensions are <term><m>\dfrac{20}{3}</m> in. by <m>\dfrac{20}{3}</m> in.</term>, creating a maximum volume of:
        <me>V\left(\dfrac{5}{3}\right) = \dfrac{5}{3}\left(\dfrac{20}{3}\right)^2 = \dfrac{2000}{27} \approx 74.07 \text{ in}^3</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 10 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-linearization-trig">
    <statement>
      <p> Approximate <m>\cos(61^\circ)</m> from <m>\cos(60^\circ)</m> using linear approximation. </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Convert values to radians: center point <m>a = 60^\circ = \dfrac{\pi}{3}</m>, 
        target point <m>x = 61^\circ = \dfrac{\pi}{3} + \dfrac{\pi}{180}</m>, 
        meaning <m>dx = \dfrac{\pi}{180}</m>.
        Let <m>f(x) = \cos x \implies f'(x) = -\sin x</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow>f(a) \amp = \cos\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right) = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
          <mrow>f'(a) \amp = -\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{3}\right) = -\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}</mrow>
        </md>
        Construct the linear tangent line: <m>L(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)</m>:
        <me>\cos(61^\circ) \approx \frac{1}{2} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\left(\frac{\pi}{180}\right) \approx 0.5 - 0.86603(0.01745) \approx 0.4849</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 11 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-lhopital">
    <introduction>
      <p> Use l'Hôpital's rule to evaluate the limit. </p>
    </introduction>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 2}\frac{x-2}{x^2-4}</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This yields an indeterminate form of type <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>. Differentiating the numerator and denominator gives:
          <me>\lim_{x\to 2}\frac{\frac{d}{dx}(x-2)}{\frac{d}{dx}(x^2-4)} = \lim_{x\to 2}\frac{1}{2x} = \frac{1}{2(2)} = \frac{1}{4}</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to -3}\frac{x^3-4x+15}{x^2-x-12}</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Direct substitution yields <m>\frac{(-3)^3-4(-3)+15}{(-3)^2-(-3)-12} = \frac{0}{0}</m>. Differentiate via L'Hôpital's rule:
          <me>\lim_{x\to -3}\frac{3x^2-4}{2x-1} = \frac{3(-3)^2-4}{2(-3)-1} = \frac{27-4}{-6-1} = -\frac{23}{7}</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to \infty}\frac{5x^3-2x}{7x^3+3}</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This yields the form <m>\frac{\infty}{\infty}</m>. Differentiating three consecutive times or checking dominant trailing polynomial coefficients yields:
          <me>\lim_{x\to \infty}\frac{15x^2-2}{21x^2} \implies \lim_{x\to \infty}\frac{30x}{42x} = \frac{30}{42} = \frac{5}{7}</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task>
      <statement>
        <p> <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0^1}(1+x)^{1/x}</m> </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          This is an indeterminate power form <m>1^\infty</m>. Let <m>y = (1+x)^{1/x}</m>, taking the natural log gives <m>\ln y = \frac{\ln(1+x)}{x}</m>.
          Evaluate the limit using L'Hôpital's rule for <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>:
          <me>\lim_{x\to 0^+}\frac{\ln(1+x)}{x} = \lim_{x\to 0^+}\frac{\frac{1}{1+x}}{1} = 1</me>
          Since <m>\lim \ln y = 1</m>, raising back over base <m>e</m> yields <m>e^1 = e</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 12 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-newtons-method">
    <statement>
      <p> Use Newton's method to estimate the solution of the equation <m>x^2+x-1=0</m>. Start with <m>x_0=-1</m> to find <m>x_2</m>. </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x) = x^2 + x - 1 \implies f'(x) = 2x + 1</m>. The iterative formula is <m>x_{n+1} = x_n - \frac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow>x_1 \amp = x_0 - \frac{f(-1)}{f'(-1)} = -1 - \frac{(-1)^2 + (-1) - 1}{2(-1) + 1} = -1 - \frac{-1}{-1} = -2</mrow>
          <mrow>x_2 \amp = x_1 - \frac{f(-2)}{f'(-2)} = -2 - \frac{(-2)^2 + (-2) - 1}{2(-2) + 1} = -2 - \frac{1}{-3} = -2 + \frac{1}{3} = -\frac{5}{3} \approx -1.667</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 13 -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-definite-integral">
    <statement>
      <p> Evaluate <m>\displaystyle\int^2_1 \dfrac{5~dx}{x}</m> </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Factor out the constant and integrate:
        <me>\int^2_1 \frac{5}{x}\,dx = 5\left[\ln|x|\right]^2_1 = 5(\ln 2 - \ln 1) = 5\ln 2 \approx 3.465</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- Problem 14 -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-fill-in-blank-derivatives-integrals">
    <introduction>
      <p> For each given function, determine its derivative and its general indefinite integral. </p>
    </introduction>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Complete the table below by filling in the missing derivative and indefinite integral formulas:
      </p>
      <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row header="yes">
          <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[f(x)\right]</m> <m>\qquad\qquad</m> </cell>
          <cell><m>\displaystyle\int f(x)\,dx + C</m> <m>\qquad\qquad</m> </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>x^n \quad (n \neq -1)</m><m>\rule{0pt}{3ex}</m></cell>
          <cell> <m>\qquad\qquad</m> </cell>
          <cell> <m>\qquad\qquad</m> </cell>
        </row>
        <row fill="colortheme">
          <cell><m>\sin 2x</m><m>\rule{0pt}{4ex}</m></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\cos 3x</m><m>\rule{0pt}{4ex}</m></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row fill="colortheme">
          <cell><m>\tan x</m><m>\rule{0pt}{4ex}</m></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>e^{-x}</m><m>\rule{0pt}{4ex}</m></cell>
          <cell></cell>
          <cell></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The completed table values are evaluated as follows:
      </p>
      <tabular halign="center" bottom="minor" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <row header="yes">
          <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[f(x)\right]</m> <m>\qquad\qquad</m> </cell>
          <cell><m>\displaystyle\int f(x)\,dx + C</m> <m>\qquad\qquad</m> </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>x^n \quad (n \neq -1)</m>  <m>\rule{0pt}{5ex}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>n x^{n-1}</m> </cell>
          <cell><m>\dfrac{x^{n+1}}{n+1} + C</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row fill="colortheme">
          <cell><m>\sin 2x</m><m>\rule{0pt}{5ex}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>2\cos 2x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-\dfrac{1}{2}\cos 2x + C</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\cos 3x</m><m>\rule{0pt}{5ex}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-3\sin 3x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{3}\sin 3x + C</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row fill="colortheme">
          <cell><m>\tan x</m><m>\rule{0pt}{5ex}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\sec^2 x</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\ln|\sec x| + C</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>e^{-x}</m><m>\rule{0pt}{5ex}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-e^{-x}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-e^{-x} + C</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</exercises>

</section>